[h1]OpenZone Storage[/h1]
Virtual storage boxes for the OpenZone series: a box whose contents do not exist in the game world while it is closed, so the loot of a closed box is not in the world at all. Part of the OpenZone series, built for the [UA] STALKER: Zone Protocol server.

[b]What you get[/b]
[list]
[*]Three boxes from three kits: Small (250 cells, 2 weapon slots, a wooden crate), Medium (500 cells, 4 slots, a sea chest) and Large (1000 cells, 6 slots, a sea chest). A kit is deployed from the hands: pick the spot, turn the hologram with the wheel, hold the action for ten seconds.
[*]One verb on the box, "Look in the box". The placed box is only the anchor you walk up to: its contents are read from the bridge's database into a container nobody is told about, streamed to your screen, and every drag is one operation the server performs and writes before the next. The box is shown to whoever presses the verb; the session ends when the last of them closes the screen, walks off (six metres), leaves or dies. Nothing is on a timer.
[*]The inventory works across the box's edge the way vanilla's does: two stacks merge when one is dragged onto the other (from a pocket or off the ground, in either direction), a loose item dropped on an item in the box changes places with it, and [b]Alt+click[/b] moves an item from the box into your inventory or from your inventory (hands included) into the box.
[*]A search bar that shades every item whose name does not match and keeps a container lit if anything inside it matches, however deep; a Sort button that lays the box out by name; a Count button that lists what the box holds, in total and by class.
[*]Opening reads the items back paced at 500 a second and 5 ms of a frame at most, and the box's record is written on the same budget: a thousand-item box costs no frame more than that.
[*][b]The personal stash.[/b] An admin places a locker -- a placed item like a box, saved with the world. A player presses F on it and is shown a record of their own, keyed by which locker and whose: the same player at two lockers has two stashes, and one locker holds one per player. It carries the character's own slots -- head, face, eyes, body, vest, back, belt, legs, feet, gloves, armband, shoulder, melee -- and four weapon slots, so a whole kit hangs up instead of being piled into a grid, and clothing hung there keeps working pockets. Nothing stands in the world for it: the record goes to that one player's screen and nowhere else, and the pairing of locker and player is made on the server from who sent the message, so nobody at the same locker can name yours.
[*]An admin web page served by the bridge: box lists, contents, history and rollback, and three live commands to the running game (close, remove, report); signing in through Discord is optional.
[/list]
[b]Requires[/b] [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=1559212036]Community Framework[/url], [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3798432022]OpenZone Core[/url], and the OpenZone bridge [b]0.5.0 or newer[/b] running on the server ([url=https://github.com/OpenZone-DayZ/openzone-bridge]github.com/OpenZone-DayZ/openzone-bridge[/url]); the setup is described on the GitHub page below.
[b]For server owners[/b] The kits are not in the shipped economy: an example types.xml lists all six classes at nominal 0, so you decide how kits reach players.
[b]Source code[/b] [url=https://github.com/OpenZone-DayZ/openzone-storage]github.com/OpenZone-DayZ/openzone-storage[/url]

[b]Licence, repacking, selling[/b]
You may repack this mod into your server's own pack, keeping the attribution and the same licence. You may not sell it, sublicense it or include it in any paid bundle. Running it on a server that accepts donations, sells cosmetics or priority queue slots is fine. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 with the additional permission in NOTICE.

